# Build context should be exactly what the Dockerfile's `COPY . .` needs to
# compile ./cmd/airbg: go.mod, go.sum, cmd/, internal/ (including the
# go:embed'd migrations under internal/db/migrations). Everything below is
# plainly not a build input and would otherwise be shipped to the daemon on
# every build.

.git
.github
.superpowers
.claude

# Legacy PHP app — the Go image has no use for it.
www-root

# Local secrets. .env.example tells the operator to `cp .env.example .env`, and
# .env then holds a real AIRBG_DATABASE_URL. The build stage is `COPY . .`, so
# without this the credential lands in that layer: the shipped distroless image
# stays clean (it copies only the binary and airbg.yaml), but an exported or
# pushed build cache carries it. .env is not a build input under any
# circumstances — configuration reaches the container at run time.
.env
.env.*

# Docs, planning artifacts, and local-only files.
*.md
docker-compose.yml
.gitignore

# A locally built binary (.gitignore's /airbg). Never a build input: the image
# builds its own inside the golang stage, and shipping a host-built one would
# make the image's contents depend on the developer's machine.
/airbg

# The image builds these itself (stage `web` builds the frontend, stage
# `build` embeds its output). A developer's local, possibly-stale copy must
# never reach the daemon: the whole point of the multi-stage build is that
# the shipped dist/ matches the sources being built, not whatever happened
# to be on disk when `docker build` ran.
web/node_modules/
internal/web/dist/
